1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
If digging ditches is your game, these machines are the biggest and baddest shovels in the pit.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:14,000
From the legendary steam shovel, to super shovels with teeth, and monster shovels with bigger teeth.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
The latest scoops are stronger, faster, and even smarter than ever.

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, shovels dig it on Modern Marbles.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,600
Consider the shovel.

6
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:41,340
It may seem uncomplicated, just a blade on a stick.

7
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:46,660
Yet it's actually a precision-designed alloy steel tool that's built to last.

8
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:52,900
They're stamped out by the millions, in dozens of shapes and styles for every possible task.

9
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:59,120
Yet the venerable hand shovel that helped create civilization is limited by the strength of its user.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,200
But when human strength is taken out of the equation, the shovel can get bigger, much bigger.

11
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:13,940
Meet the P&H Mining 4100A Electric Mining Shovel.

12
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:19,860
U.S. Borax operates two of them.

13
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:26,020
And if moving rock and dirt in a hurry is the game plan, nothing else even comes close.

14
00:01:26,021 --> 00:01:32,380
From the ground to the tip of its massive boom, this shovel stands seven stories tall.

15
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:35,280
Its dipper weighs 85 tons.

16
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:41,560
We run our 4,100s 24 hours a day, seven days a week, 354 days a year.

17
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:47,060
We typically get 20 hours of operating time out of a shovel each day.

18
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:50,020
It's typically scooping up a little over 80 tons.

19
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,740
You can put three dippers in a haul truck in just a little over a minute.

20
00:01:56,020 --> 00:02:02,120
In 24 hours' time, these shovels will load 140,000 tons apiece.

21
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:07,680
This is California's largest surface mine, operated by U.S. Borax.

22
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:12,680
Borax is a major ingredient in hundreds of industrial and household products.

23
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:18,040
Beginning in 1919, the mine was underground.

24
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:20,240
But today, it's an open pit.

25
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:25,500
And getting to the Borax means digging out 50 million tons of overburden per year.

26
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:30,400
That's mining talk for dirt, rock, and other materials covering a precious mineral deposit.

27
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:33,360
At U.S.

28
00:02:33,361 --> 00:02:39,760
Borax and hundreds of other coal, copper, gold, and iron mines, quotas must be met.

29
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:42,080
So the speed of the digging is critical.

30
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,600
But with a machine this big, the headaches are supersized.

31
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:49,600
We worry about where to tear on the shovel.

32
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:52,840
We worry about centering the loads on the truck.

33
00:02:52,841 --> 00:02:54,000
That's a big issue.

34
00:02:54,001 --> 00:02:58,000
I also worry about my support equipment.

35
00:02:58,001 --> 00:03:00,000
I worry about my power cable behind me.

36
00:03:00,001 --> 00:03:04,000
Powered the shovel with 7,200 AC volts.

37
00:03:04,001 --> 00:03:08,000
The shovel operator is in a very critical position.

38
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:11,000
There's a lot going on. The bank's changing.

39
00:03:11,001 --> 00:03:16,000
There's huge trucks backing in on either side of them or her.

40
00:03:16,001 --> 00:03:20,000
The other thing is an operator is under tremendous pressure to produce.

41
00:03:20,001 --> 00:03:23,000
These machines make a lot of money and dig a lot of material.

42
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Constant digging and swinging puts tremendous strain on the shovels.

43
00:03:29,001 --> 00:03:34,000
That's where the two and a half inch diameter steel cables come in.

44
00:03:34,001 --> 00:03:41,000
This is a wire rope shovel, meaning the high tensile cables do the heavy lifting.

45
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,000
The digging starts when a pinion moves the dipper handle and the dipper away from the boom arm and into the digging face.

46
00:03:52,001 --> 00:03:54,452
face. This is called crowding.

47
00:03:54,453 --> 00:03:59,600
Then a rope drum reeling in the hoist cable rakes the dipper up

48
00:03:59,601 --> 00:04:00,906
the side of the face.

49
00:04:00,907 --> 00:04:05,320
When the dipper is full, the machine's upper half swings into position,

50
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:10,915
a latch bar releases the dipper door, and the material falls out.

51
00:04:10,916 --> 00:04:12,980
A hydraulic snubber or brake

52
00:04:12,981 --> 00:04:17,631
keeps the heavy door from slamming shut and damaging the dipper.

53
00:04:17,632 --> 00:04:19,520
In any high-capacity mine,

54
00:04:19,521 --> 00:04:22,060
these digging cycles are repeated endlessly,

55
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:24,700
an open invitation for human error.

56
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:27,200
They call it boom jacking.

57
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:28,880
When they're digging with the dipper,

58
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,640
they can actually jack the boom up.

59
00:04:32,340 --> 00:04:35,840
And if they overcrowd, the boom can lift up slightly,

60
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:37,180
and when it comes back down,

61
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:40,480
tremendous forces are put into the shovel itself.

62
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:46,620
So operators have to continually monitor what they're doing.

63
00:04:46,621 --> 00:04:50,780
You hit a rock, you hit a hard place in the ground, jacks a boom.

64
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:55,440
It shuts the shovel down, locks all brakes.

65
00:04:56,280 --> 00:05:02,980
You have to restart it and also logs in on the computer that there's been a stage one boom jack.

66
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:09,380
Advanced software packages are helping operators prevent boom jacking before it causes catastrophic damage.

67
00:05:09,381 --> 00:05:16,760
Despite its awesome capacity and $12 million price tag,

68
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:21,400
this shovel bears a striking resemblance to a machine from another age.

69
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:27,860
In 1835, a construction contractor named William Smith Otis

70
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:30,520
built the world's first steam-powered shovel.

71
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:35,020
It was mounted on railroad rails with small little wheels.

72
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:37,360
It had no house on it.

73
00:05:37,361 --> 00:05:39,920
It was just a bare-bone structure with a steam engine,

74
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:44,060
and it had a boom up front and a dipper handle and a dipper

75
00:05:44,061 --> 00:05:52,600
that in its basic crude form still looks pretty much like the electric mining shovels of today.

76
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:58,620
But the Otis shovel was ahead of its time, and hand labor was plentiful and cheap.

77
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:05,680
Four decades later in the 1870s, rapid industrialization and the expanding railroads

78
00:06:05,681 --> 00:06:08,320
heralded the arrival of the steam shovel era.

79
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:12,640
Two companies emerged from a crowd of competitors

80
00:06:12,641 --> 00:06:15,540
to define the shovel for the next century and beyond.

81
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:19,020
The Bucyrus Steam Shovel and Dredge Company

82
00:06:19,021 --> 00:06:21,480
and the Marion Steam Shovel Company.

83
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:25,820
Both entered the shovel-building race at the same time.

84
00:06:25,821 --> 00:06:30,820
The Barnhart shovel was introduced in Marion, Ohio in 1882,

85
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:35,040
and one year later the Marion Steam Shovel Company

86
00:06:35,041 --> 00:06:37,040
was formed to build that shovel.

87
00:06:37,041 --> 00:06:43,000
Musyris' first steam shovel was called the Thompson, and it was designed to move on rails

88
00:06:43,001 --> 00:06:49,160
for an important reason, to build railroads, one scoop at a time.

89
00:06:49,161 --> 00:06:54,760
As America's railroads pushed into the frontier, steam shovels were in the vanguard, slicing

90
00:06:54,761 --> 00:06:57,260
through mountains and filling in valleys.

91
00:06:57,261 --> 00:07:02,400
The power shovel in those early days was simply mounted on a flatbed car.

92
00:07:02,401 --> 00:07:05,040
It was at the end, literally at the end of the railroad.

93
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:07,880
It would run the railroad tracks up to the hill,

94
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:11,360
and it would actually dig in front of the railroad tracks,

95
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:17,080
and it would turn around and dump off to the side of the tracks into an adjacent track.

96
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:23,060
So as the track advanced, the shovel moved forward and just kind of dug its way through.

97
00:07:23,061 --> 00:07:27,280
There were huge crews of people who did nothing but move railroad tracks.

98
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:29,100
Much like the railroads,

99
00:07:29,101 --> 00:07:33,500
surface mining operations used steam shovels to dramatically increase productivity.

100
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:38,615
Between 1880 and 1890, U.S.

101
00:07:38,616 --> 00:07:44,280
steel production went from 1.4 million tons to more than 11 million tons.

102
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:47,211
Without these machines, the U.S.

103
00:07:47,212 --> 00:07:54,640
could not have progressed in iron and coal and copper it needed for the growth of industry in the U.S.

104
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:57,740
The machines rapidly increased in size.

105
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:02,720
Dipper capacities went from half a cubic yard to six cubic yards,

106
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:05,240
approximately nine tons of earth.

107
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:11,120
In 1888, the French gave up on their effort to complete the Panama Canal.

108
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:18,600
Sixteen years later, American steam shovels went to work on Theodore Roosevelt's grand project.

109
00:08:19,260 --> 00:08:24,320
Panama Canal project was one of the most amazing engineering feats known to mankind.

110
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:37,716
Between the years 1904 and 1914, a total of 77 Gisaris and 24 Merion power shovels were engaged in moving an incredible 255 million cubic yards of material in a very,

111
00:08:37,717 --> 00:08:41,340
very difficult place in the Isthmus of Panama.

112
00:08:41,820 --> 00:08:44,520
In the humid tropics, the work was stifling.

113
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:49,720
Huge work gangs scrambled to lay new track for the shovels to advance.

114
00:08:49,721 --> 00:08:59,060
always wary of the landslides that occasionally buried man and machine and every week the progress

115
00:08:59,061 --> 00:09:05,660
of each of the shovels were published sort of a running score sheet of which shovel dug the most

116
00:09:05,661 --> 00:09:11,280
the champion shovel of the week got to fly a broom up there that meant that was their badge of honor

117
00:09:11,281 --> 00:09:17,760
that they were the best shovel for the week on the panama canal the new century ushered in many

118
00:09:17,761 --> 00:09:23,160
steam shovel innovations. Rails began to disappear, replaced by independent

119
00:09:23,161 --> 00:09:29,500
crawler tracks. In 1914, the Ball Engine Company of Erie, Pennsylvania, began

120
00:09:29,501 --> 00:09:35,320
producing steam shovels for the small contractor. Renamed the Erie Steam

121
00:09:35,321 --> 00:09:41,500
Shovel Company, it built its first model with crawler tracks in 1921, the Erie B.

122
00:09:41,501 --> 00:09:46,540
In the farmlands of eastern Pennsylvania,

123
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:51,600
mechanic Ray Fleetwood has restored his very own 1921 Erie B shovel

124
00:09:51,601 --> 00:09:53,800
to its original working condition.

125
00:09:56,040 --> 00:10:00,980
Basically, the machine is close to the way it came out of the factory.

126
00:10:01,540 --> 00:10:07,380
It's not totally restored, but I do work on it, you know, mostly on the weekends.

127
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,440
There's still some things I want to do to it.

128
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:13,960
but it's a hobby.

129
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:20,300
The onboard crew of a vintage steam shovel includes two workers,

130
00:10:20,820 --> 00:10:22,640
the operator and the fireman.

131
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:27,480
The fireman shovels coal at intervals to maintain a hot fire.

132
00:10:29,460 --> 00:10:33,600
He also monitors the steam pressure to ensure a constant supply of power.

133
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:38,820
Fire and water are the only elements the Erie Bee needs.

134
00:10:38,821 --> 00:10:48,700
water is periodically injected from a 275 gallon tank into the hot boiler the water is converted

135
00:10:48,701 --> 00:10:55,500
to steam which creates enormous pressure a series of valves regulate the pressure as it travels to

136
00:10:55,501 --> 00:11:04,200
a main gearbox the gears transmit power to several pistons that control the dippers motions upfront

137
00:11:04,201 --> 00:11:07,760
the operator works a series of crude levers to facilitate the digging.

138
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:14,520
The lever on the right controls the speed and the function of the main engine.

139
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:22,640
The foot pedal controls the space.

140
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:23,520
It's like the brake.

141
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:27,500
It controls the speed of the bucket being dropped to the ground.

142
00:11:27,501 --> 00:11:40,898
They were small enough and inexpensive enough that a contractor could buy them and make a good living by using that shovel for city work, basements, housing, apartment

143
00:11:40,899 --> 00:11:41,700
buildings.

144
00:11:41,701 --> 00:11:50,140
Wherever you needed a small shovel that could be handled on a truck, hauled quickly from one side to the other, an eerie shovel met that need.

145
00:11:50,141 --> 00:11:59,951
In the early 1900s, power shovels were characterized by steam power, although with growing availability of other energy sources, power sources like gasoline and diesel

146
00:11:59,952 --> 00:12:07,940
and gasoline electric, diesel electric and even full electric power sources, these shovels became even more powerful and more efficient.

147
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:10,821
They also got bigger, a lot bigger.

148
00:12:10,822 --> 00:12:18,360
A new class of behemoths called stripping shovels took full advantage of electric power in mining operations.

149
00:12:18,361 --> 00:12:25,360
As early as 1914, these large strippers featured booms up to 75 feet long.

150
00:12:25,361 --> 00:12:32,360
Their purpose wasn't to load trucks, but rather to use their great reach to excavate huge strips of earth,

151
00:12:32,361 --> 00:12:35,360
in which rich coal seams were buried.

152
00:12:35,361 --> 00:12:41,360
As the Bucyrus and Marion companies scrambled to supply large mining companies with bigger machines,

153
00:12:41,361 --> 00:12:45,360
a virtual power shuttle arms race began.

154
00:12:45,361 --> 00:12:49,020
with each company upping the ante in size and dipper capacity.

155
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:57,440
In the 1950s and 60s, these super strippers became some of the largest land-based machines on the planet.

156
00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:03,980
In 1965, Marion unveiled the Captain Shovel.

157
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:13,300
Weighing a record-shattering 15,000 tons, it swung a 180-cubic-yard dipper that could hoist a 270-ton payload.

158
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:25,660
In April of 2006, the world's last operating super stripper, Osiris' Silver Spade, officially retired in Cadiz, Ohio.

159
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:31,160
The super strippers became too costly to operate and too limited in their range.

160
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:36,340
Today, the versatile walking drag line has replaced the strippers.

161
00:13:37,540 --> 00:13:44,540
These more nimble giants swing an independent bucket that can dig deeper and cast material much farther than a stripping shovel.

162
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:52,360
In 21st century surface mines, electric mining shovels have a rival.

163
00:13:52,361 --> 00:13:54,360
Hydraulic excavators.

164
00:13:54,361 --> 00:13:57,360
They don't rely on mechanically operated cables.

165
00:13:57,361 --> 00:14:06,360
Instead, hydraulic excavators use diesel engines to drive hydraulic pumps, motors, and cylinders that control the digging motions.

166
00:14:06,361 --> 00:14:11,360
They're very good machines. They have high mobility.

167
00:14:11,361 --> 00:14:19,580
Hydraulic excavators are usually equipped with diesel power so they can move from one spot to the other in the mine quite rapidly.

168
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:21,140
They're very versatile.

169
00:14:21,700 --> 00:14:29,380
After a century and a half of digging and countless configurations, the power shovel has earned a place in the pantheon of earthmovers.

170
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:33,820
But not every digging job needs extreme muscle machines.

171
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:39,320
Often, a hand shovel will do, just like it has for thousands of years.

172
00:14:39,321 --> 00:14:43,640
You won't find these strange-looking tools at the local hardware store.

173
00:14:44,220 --> 00:14:45,880
They have very specific functions.

174
00:14:46,580 --> 00:14:48,480
But yes, they are shovels.

175
00:14:50,120 --> 00:14:59,020
In 1993, the largest mining shovel ever built, the Captain, was sold for scrap after a 1991 fire caused catastrophic damage.

176
00:15:00,140 --> 00:15:04,820
To most casual users of the hand shovel, what you're seeing is what you get.

177
00:15:04,821 --> 00:15:09,940
Not much cause for celebration, let alone dedicating a museum.

178
00:15:10,660 --> 00:15:16,720
But in the historic town of Easton, Massachusetts, a shovel museum is exactly what you'll find.

179
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:24,460
The Industrial History Center at Stonehill College is the proud home of the largest collection of shovels in the world.

180
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:28,460
A snapshot of more than 200 years of American industry.

181
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:32,960
So this is one row of our shovel storage area.

182
00:15:32,961 --> 00:15:38,140
we have over 750 shovels in our collection that represent a variety of shapes and types and dates

183
00:15:38,141 --> 00:15:41,580
and manufactured brands from the Ames Company.

184
00:15:42,020 --> 00:15:47,860
In the late 18th century, the Ames Company began reinventing and refining an ancient tool.

185
00:15:49,540 --> 00:15:55,240
The company succeeded in designing and marketing a large number of not-so-typical takes on the typical shovel.

186
00:15:57,100 --> 00:16:02,300
There may be hundreds of shovel types in the museum, but they all rely on the same physics.

187
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:06,454
A shovel is actually three tools in one.

188
00:16:06,455 --> 00:16:10,560
A handle or lever, a scoop, and a cutting blade

189
00:16:10,561 --> 00:16:11,837
or inclined plane.

190
00:16:11,838 --> 00:16:16,660
The blade cuts into the soil by concentrating power at the sharpened

191
00:16:16,661 --> 00:16:21,882
edge. The handle provides leverage for prying out the material.

192
00:16:21,883 --> 00:16:24,120
When digging, the soil acts

193
00:16:24,121 --> 00:16:29,340
as the fulcrum. The longer the handle, the greater the leverage.

194
00:16:29,341 --> 00:16:31,460
When lifting the material,

195
00:16:31,461 --> 00:16:33,320
a worker's hand becomes the fulcrum.

196
00:16:34,760 --> 00:16:37,520
Depending on the density of the material a worker digs,

197
00:16:38,140 --> 00:16:41,800
the blade's angle or lift is either increased or decreased

198
00:16:41,801 --> 00:16:43,980
to deliver the best position of attack.

199
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:46,940
Good shovel design didn't occur overnight.

200
00:16:47,540 --> 00:16:50,980
The very first digging tools that would have been used

201
00:16:50,981 --> 00:16:52,920
in a kind of shovel-like capacity,

202
00:16:53,280 --> 00:16:57,280
we can go all the way back to, gosh, over 100,000 years ago

203
00:16:57,281 --> 00:17:03,720
to some really well-preserved examples of wooden digging sticks from cave sites in Africa.

204
00:17:04,220 --> 00:17:07,640
When they were sharpened at one end and probably fire-hardened,

205
00:17:07,740 --> 00:17:11,480
placed in the fire to harden the tip a bit and then used for a variety of things

206
00:17:11,481 --> 00:17:16,040
to dig up grubs, to dig up root crops, to do all kinds of stuff.

207
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:20,700
As human beings discovered agriculture roughly 10,000 years ago,

208
00:17:21,380 --> 00:17:25,180
a crude shovel with a separate handle or haft became a hot commodity.

209
00:17:25,181 --> 00:17:29,680
One of the first things that we see is even in those simple digging sticks, we find people

210
00:17:29,681 --> 00:17:36,280
putting part of a large mammal bone on the end of it to reinforce that end of a digging

211
00:17:36,281 --> 00:17:38,120
stick to make it harder.

212
00:17:38,121 --> 00:17:42,800
But in terms of hafted components, we've got everything from shell to bone.

213
00:17:42,801 --> 00:17:48,260
The scapular or shoulder blade of a bison is a real common hafted blade for an early

214
00:17:48,261 --> 00:17:50,720
agricultural implement.

215
00:17:50,721 --> 00:17:56,220
This is a stone blade that would have been hafted to a wooden handle and would have been

216
00:17:56,221 --> 00:17:59,440
used to excavate the earth, to shovel the earth.

217
00:17:59,441 --> 00:18:04,960
One of the characteristics that's very nice to see is the very high polish or sheen on

218
00:18:04,961 --> 00:18:09,780
the working tip of the tool that would have been the result of this coming in contact

219
00:18:09,781 --> 00:18:11,620
repeatedly with the soil.

220
00:18:11,621 --> 00:18:15,220
And for archaeologists, that's a real nice sign that it's been used to shovel or excavate

221
00:18:15,221 --> 00:18:17,220
soils.

222
00:18:17,221 --> 00:18:23,260
As metallurgy progressed more than 5,000 years ago, the first blacksmiths began forging tools

223
00:18:23,261 --> 00:18:26,020
that more closely resembled today's shovels and spades.

224
00:18:27,780 --> 00:18:32,240
Rome may not have been built in a day, but it was probably built with shovels.

225
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:35,860
Most were made of wood and some of forged iron.

226
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:39,620
But iron was often scarce and expensive.

227
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:45,800
So for centuries, many common shovels were wooden, with a thin band of forged, sharpened

228
00:18:45,801 --> 00:18:46,960
iron at the leading edge.

229
00:18:47,220 --> 00:18:51,840
This hybrid wood and iron-rimmed shovel survived even into colonial America.

230
00:18:53,760 --> 00:18:58,780
But for working blacksmiths in the colonies, making a shovel was technically illegal.

231
00:18:59,540 --> 00:19:04,100
The typical way a colony worked was it provided raw materials for the mother country.

232
00:19:04,520 --> 00:19:06,940
They generally weren't supposed to be making finished goods.

233
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:11,200
So there were English rules imposed on America that we weren't supposed to have ironworks.

234
00:19:11,201 --> 00:19:21,720
In 1774, a Massachusetts blacksmith named Captain John Ames began churning out muskets and illegal hybrid shovels in large numbers.

235
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:25,220
Both implements were likely used to fight the British.

236
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:32,860
When Captain Ames' son, Oliver, took over in 1803, he moved the company to Easton, Massachusetts.

237
00:19:33,800 --> 00:19:36,040
He was obsessed with a single tool.

238
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:39,440
Oliver Ames, who is the focus of our collection,

239
00:19:39,620 --> 00:19:44,160
was one of the first in America to focus on large quantity production of iron shovels,

240
00:19:44,200 --> 00:19:47,260
all iron shovels, not just wooden shovels with an iron edge.

241
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:51,600
Ames' legacy as the oldest continuously operating business in America

242
00:19:51,601 --> 00:19:55,320
is preserved by the museum at Easton Stonehill College.

243
00:19:56,820 --> 00:19:58,940
Ames' shovels took the lead in building America.

244
00:19:59,720 --> 00:20:04,320
Beginning in the 1820s, the Erie Canal and other smaller canal projects

245
00:20:04,321 --> 00:20:06,800
spiked the demand for his well-made product.

246
00:20:08,440 --> 00:20:11,200
Urban construction, road building, agriculture,

247
00:20:11,460 --> 00:20:14,180
and even the California gold rush of 1849

248
00:20:14,181 --> 00:20:16,720
kept Ames' factories working overtime.

249
00:20:18,940 --> 00:20:23,360
In the 1860s, the expanding railroads required armies of immigrants,

250
00:20:23,700 --> 00:20:25,000
all wielding shovels.

251
00:20:26,880 --> 00:20:29,720
Rapidly increasing demand allowed the Ames company

252
00:20:29,721 --> 00:20:33,080
to become the 800-pound gorilla of shovel manufacturers.

253
00:20:33,081 --> 00:20:37,240
By the late 19th century, they were the largest shovel maker in the world.

254
00:20:38,180 --> 00:20:44,900
And there were consolidation periods in the 1890s, 1910, and then 1920, 1930,

255
00:20:45,080 --> 00:20:50,140
in which they purchased and merged with other shovel making companies and just sort of got bigger and bigger.

256
00:20:50,640 --> 00:20:54,800
Ames pioneered unique and even bizarre shovel designs for countless markets.

257
00:20:55,480 --> 00:21:03,940
This is a potato scoop, and it's designed to let the dirt fall through and keep what you want to keep, which is the potatoes.

258
00:21:04,580 --> 00:21:06,080
It's a very beautiful-looking shovel.

259
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:10,300
We have this sort of strange two-handled shovel that we don't know a lot about.

260
00:21:10,700 --> 00:21:17,180
It looks like this may have been a prototype, maybe to unload a wagon or something like that where you need some different leverage points.

261
00:21:17,740 --> 00:21:20,580
So this is what's called a forking spade.

262
00:21:20,581 --> 00:21:23,920
It's some combination of a pitchfork and a shovel.

263
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:28,140
He's for rotating crops or turning over soil and things like that.

264
00:21:28,600 --> 00:21:31,640
So here's a series of shovels, and most of these are coffee bean scoops.

265
00:21:32,340 --> 00:21:34,660
I mean, who thinks that you need special shovels for coffee beans?

266
00:21:34,820 --> 00:21:41,160
But it's an interesting design for rotating the beans or for just sort of using it more like a paddle.

267
00:21:41,720 --> 00:21:44,100
So these are some of the larger shovels in our collection.

268
00:21:44,260 --> 00:21:45,580
They're about 10 feet long.

269
00:21:46,060 --> 00:21:51,740
This shovel with a square blade is a baker's shovel used for working in a long baking oven.

270
00:21:51,741 --> 00:21:55,740
This other shovel is a telegraph spoon.

271
00:21:55,741 --> 00:22:00,740
That would be used for digging a telephone pole or telegraph pole hole.

272
00:22:00,741 --> 00:22:03,740
So here's a shovel that at first glance you might think is very old.

273
00:22:03,741 --> 00:22:06,740
It's a wooden shovel. Why would we be making wooden shovels in modern times?

274
00:22:06,741 --> 00:22:08,740
But in fact, this is a World War II snow shovel.

275
00:22:08,741 --> 00:22:12,740
Iron and steel in World War II are being used for more important things like tanks.

276
00:22:12,741 --> 00:22:17,740
So in a shovel where you could get away with less iron or steel, you took that advantage.

277
00:22:17,741 --> 00:22:20,740
So here is sort of a throwback to an ancient design.

278
00:22:21,740 --> 00:22:24,660
But Ames wasn't just making shovels for the home front.

279
00:22:25,620 --> 00:22:28,600
Their tools were used in every war ever fought by Americans.

280
00:22:29,900 --> 00:22:33,980
After the rifle, a short-handled shovel was a soldier's best friend.

281
00:22:35,760 --> 00:22:41,660
In World War II, Ames resorted to espionage to create its famous folding entrenching tool.

282
00:22:41,661 --> 00:22:45,100
The Germans innovated the concept of a folding shovel.

283
00:22:45,580 --> 00:22:49,600
And the German shovel was captured and sent back here to Easton for the Ames Company to copy.

284
00:22:49,601 --> 00:22:57,940
And this is a 1943 Ames entrenching tool with the design that people are probably more familiar with that folds.

285
00:22:59,360 --> 00:23:06,820
And Ames continues to make and continue to make these shovels and continue to make enhancements and innovations.

286
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:10,480
So this is a Vietnam-era folding entrenching tool.

287
00:23:10,600 --> 00:23:13,260
So we can demonstrate here how it would fold up.

288
00:23:13,360 --> 00:23:15,060
This one actually has a pick on it as well.

289
00:23:15,061 --> 00:23:22,060
The world's oldest manufacturer of shovels is still supplying the military and civilians with dozens of modern shovel designs.

290
00:23:22,061 --> 00:23:26,060
It's now called the Ames True Temper Company.

291
00:23:26,061 --> 00:23:30,060
But today, Ames has plenty of competition.

292
00:23:30,061 --> 00:23:40,060
One shovel maker uses sharp teeth and 21st century materials to build what it claims is the world's greatest shovel.

293
00:23:40,061 --> 00:23:48,060
The Ames Shovel and Tool Company produced more than 11 million entrenching shovels during World War II.

294
00:23:48,061 --> 00:23:52,060
Forget about shoveling snow or digging ditches.

295
00:23:52,061 --> 00:23:55,060
This shovel is built for speed.

296
00:23:55,061 --> 00:24:05,060
Every year since 1975, the World Shovel Race Championship has drawn hundreds of Blade Runners to determine who rides the swiftest scoop.

297
00:24:05,061 --> 00:24:15,020
But because an off-the-shelf grain shovel has a limited top speed, the event welcomes fully tricked out, customized shovel sleds, part of the official modified shovel

298
00:24:15,021 --> 00:24:15,721
division.

299
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:19,303
The only rule?

300
00:24:19,304 --> 00:24:23,880
The vehicle must be built around a shovel blade that contacts the snow.

301
00:24:23,881 --> 00:24:31,880
These guys may use science to make shovels faster, but in 1911, a researcher named Frederick

302
00:24:31,881 --> 00:24:35,340
Winslow Taylor used science to make shovels more efficient.

303
00:24:36,440 --> 00:24:40,200
Taylor published a groundbreaking book, The Principles of Scientific Management.

304
00:24:41,220 --> 00:24:45,460
His research involved studying the efficiency of various labor activities in a steel mill.

305
00:24:46,860 --> 00:24:49,680
He called one of the studies the science of shoveling.

306
00:24:49,681 --> 00:24:52,560
clipboard and stopwatch in hand,

307
00:24:53,320 --> 00:24:54,800
Taylor carefully observed workers

308
00:24:54,801 --> 00:24:57,000
to determine what could make them more productive

309
00:24:57,001 --> 00:24:59,040
and suffer fewer injuries.

310
00:24:59,360 --> 00:25:02,020
If you pick up too much material in your shovel,

311
00:25:02,120 --> 00:25:03,460
you get tired really fast.

312
00:25:03,820 --> 00:25:06,380
On the other hand, if you pick up too light of load,

313
00:25:06,500 --> 00:25:07,660
you're just wasting motions.

314
00:25:08,060 --> 00:25:10,720
So Taylor, through his calculations and measurements,

315
00:25:10,820 --> 00:25:13,140
concluded that 21 pounds is the optimal weight

316
00:25:13,141 --> 00:25:15,820
in which a shovel should carry a load.

317
00:25:15,821 --> 00:25:17,900
With 21 pounds, you're not wasting energy

318
00:25:17,901 --> 00:25:19,240
and not getting tired too fast.

319
00:25:19,680 --> 00:25:26,960
The 21-pound rule and a number of other shoveling recommendations are considered some of the first successful ergonomic studies in history.

320
00:25:29,500 --> 00:25:33,940
Taylor's findings prove that matching the correct size and style of shovel is half the work.

321
00:25:36,740 --> 00:25:40,940
In the shovel aisle of today's hardware store, Taylor's study has come full circle.

322
00:25:40,941 --> 00:25:50,180
different carbon steel alloys blade thicknesses grip styles and composite or wood handles can

323
00:25:50,181 --> 00:25:57,020
make purchasing a shovel seem like more work than actually using one the main thing that's

324
00:25:57,021 --> 00:26:01,520
changed on shovels over the years has been the method of manufacturing and how they're made

325
00:26:01,521 --> 00:26:07,820
originally they were individual tools made by blacksmiths and blacksmith shops and of course

326
00:26:07,821 --> 00:26:11,120
Now they're stamped out very rapidly.

327
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:16,140
The Seymour Manufacturing Company in Seymour, Indiana,

328
00:26:16,520 --> 00:26:18,980
set out to produce the world's greatest shovel.

329
00:26:19,980 --> 00:26:23,140
This is our Structron line of shovels.

330
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:27,540
It's a premium quality, top of the line, we say the world's best tools.

331
00:26:28,420 --> 00:26:29,880
This is probably the most common.

332
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:31,200
It's a round point shovel.

333
00:26:31,340 --> 00:26:32,860
It's primarily used for digging.

334
00:26:32,861 --> 00:26:42,860
The square point shovel is primarily used to move loose material like sand and gravel.

335
00:26:42,861 --> 00:26:48,860
Many of Seymour's shovels are made with high quality northern ash wood handles.

336
00:26:48,861 --> 00:26:55,860
They start as milled 2x2s and then are machine-lathed into rounded handles.

337
00:26:55,861 --> 00:27:04,740
But for some shovelers, stronger and lighter weight fiberglass is the preferred high-tech

338
00:27:04,741 --> 00:27:05,740
upgrade.

339
00:27:05,741 --> 00:27:11,420
To achieve its lighter weight, the Structron handle is hollow.

340
00:27:11,421 --> 00:27:17,540
The tube is composed of a fiberglass mat, surrounded by millions of fiberglass filaments.

341
00:27:17,541 --> 00:27:23,740
The shovel head itself is a heat-treated carbon fiber alloy steel that flexes but resists

342
00:27:23,741 --> 00:27:25,231
bending or breaking.

343
00:27:25,232 --> 00:27:31,120
A solid fiberglass reinforcing rod extends 12 and a half inches from the handle

344
00:27:31,121 --> 00:27:36,424
into the shank, offering maximum strength at this critical stress point.

345
00:27:36,425 --> 00:27:38,340
It takes nearly 350 pounds

346
00:27:38,341 --> 00:27:42,453
of direct bending force to break the shovel.

347
00:27:42,454 --> 00:27:47,220
And we try to balance it out so that we're not sure

348
00:27:47,221 --> 00:27:51,340
whether it's going to bend here at the socket or whether it's going to break here at the handle

349
00:27:51,341 --> 00:27:54,940
because it's balanced out so that we try to get the maximum.

350
00:27:55,700 --> 00:27:59,820
One intimidating member of the Structron family adds bite to its business.

351
00:28:00,580 --> 00:28:02,740
This is our exclusive super shovel.

352
00:28:02,980 --> 00:28:04,560
It has the teeth on the end.

353
00:28:04,660 --> 00:28:06,100
It's extra strong.

354
00:28:06,220 --> 00:28:09,280
It's good for digging in hard clay soils or rocky soils.

355
00:28:09,540 --> 00:28:11,880
If you hit roots, they won't slide off.

356
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:16,140
They'll chop the root instead of sliding off like a round-point shovel might do.

357
00:28:17,460 --> 00:28:20,660
Today's shovel manufacturing involves a cookie-cutter approach.

358
00:28:21,340 --> 00:28:25,020
The super shovel starts as a roll of raw steel.

359
00:28:26,380 --> 00:28:29,760
A 250-ton punch press stamps out the blanks.

360
00:28:32,860 --> 00:28:38,440
A 105-ton first-form press smashes the blank into a shovel head.

361
00:28:40,740 --> 00:28:46,880
One blow forms the scoop and the step, the bent or rolled edge where the shoveler places a foot.

362
00:28:46,881 --> 00:28:51,660
A second press forms the rounded socket that will accept the handle.

363
00:28:55,060 --> 00:29:00,240
Each shovel head is heat-treated for strength and then painted for corrosion resistance.

364
00:29:01,760 --> 00:29:04,840
But the super shovel isn't the only digging tool in the shed.

365
00:29:05,720 --> 00:29:10,580
This is a rice shovel used for rice farming and irrigation work.

366
00:29:10,581 --> 00:29:12,420
You'll notice the holes in here.

367
00:29:12,421 --> 00:29:17,140
They help break the suction when you're in a wet, muddy environment.

368
00:29:17,480 --> 00:29:21,280
This is a steelhead general purpose scoop.

369
00:29:21,540 --> 00:29:23,420
This is a steelhead coal scoop.

370
00:29:24,040 --> 00:29:30,120
This is an aluminum head grain scoop, and this is a polyhead grain scoop hanging here.

371
00:29:30,860 --> 00:29:35,280
This interesting one here with the teeth on the end is a roofing spade,

372
00:29:35,640 --> 00:29:41,140
and it has a fulcrum here on the back, so if you are getting your shingles up,

373
00:29:41,141 --> 00:29:45,940
You get nails, you can raise them up, raise both the shingles and the nails using that fulcrum.

374
00:29:46,340 --> 00:29:47,480
This is a fire shovel.

375
00:29:48,020 --> 00:29:52,740
The Forest Service likes these for fighting forest fires because they're small and compact

376
00:29:52,741 --> 00:29:57,300
and they don't need to be carrying a lot of extra weight when they're fighting a forest fire.

377
00:29:58,100 --> 00:30:00,060
These are obviously snow shovels.

378
00:30:00,120 --> 00:30:06,440
This is most everybody recognizes this traditional snow shovel where you actually are lifting the snow.

379
00:30:07,100 --> 00:30:10,660
For most shovelers, there's little love for the notorious snow shovel.

380
00:30:10,661 --> 00:30:15,760
Effective as it may be, using one is literally a pain in the back.

381
00:30:17,160 --> 00:30:23,520
A company called Structured Solutions 2 has reinvented the wheel, or more precisely, the snow shovel.

382
00:30:24,740 --> 00:30:28,760
The Wovle assigns most of the back-breaking work to a large wheel.

383
00:30:29,840 --> 00:30:36,200
The axle acts as an adjustable fulcrum point, and the user gets a mechanical advantage without the sore back.

384
00:30:36,201 --> 00:30:41,680
Whether it's a shovel with a wheel or a super shovel with sharp teeth,

385
00:30:42,480 --> 00:30:45,140
today's models have evolved to make the work easier.

386
00:30:46,100 --> 00:30:47,820
But if you think these teeth are impressive,

387
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:52,740
just imagine what a whole set of these 200-pound choppers can do.

388
00:30:54,120 --> 00:30:57,920
Each year, tens of thousands of Americans suffer back injuries,

389
00:30:58,240 --> 00:31:02,460
rotator cuff tears, wrist strains, and broken bones, all from shoveling snow.

390
00:31:02,461 --> 00:31:08,159
The P&H 4100A electric mining shovel rules the U.S.

391
00:31:08,160 --> 00:31:12,740
borax mine in California's Mojave Desert.

392
00:31:14,100 --> 00:31:18,820
But its birth out of raw steel and precision design may be the true marvel.

393
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:24,140
With their longtime manufacturing hubs in Milwaukee, Wisconsin,

394
00:31:25,540 --> 00:31:29,180
P&H Mining Company and its main rival, the Bucyrus Company,

395
00:31:29,181 --> 00:31:32,460
play Dr. Frankenstein to these monster shovels.

396
00:31:33,580 --> 00:31:35,440
In the vast fabrication buildings,

397
00:31:36,140 --> 00:31:39,960
the shovels begin as complex jigsaw puzzles of high-tensile steel.

398
00:31:40,540 --> 00:31:42,000
We start out with raw material.

399
00:31:42,120 --> 00:31:46,580
We cut it out, bend it, burn it into the shapes that can be welded together,

400
00:31:46,940 --> 00:31:50,260
or we put it on a machine tool and turn it into a gear or a shaft.

401
00:31:51,120 --> 00:31:55,740
Hundreds of shovel components must be cut to tolerances of thousandths of an inch,

402
00:31:55,741 --> 00:32:05,140
A necessity because moving parts must stand up to the rigors of 24-hour-a-day mining.

403
00:32:05,141 --> 00:32:08,960
But making tough shovel components also means heat treating.

404
00:32:08,961 --> 00:32:12,740
That's heating steel and then rapidly cooling it.

405
00:32:12,741 --> 00:32:17,240
Shaping, welding, and machining the pieces of a single electric mining shovel can take

406
00:32:17,241 --> 00:32:19,860
five months.

407
00:32:19,861 --> 00:32:24,460
When the digging begins, all eyes are on the business end of the shovel.

408
00:32:24,461 --> 00:32:29,580
It's called the dipper, and while it seems little more than a large metal box, it's loaded

409
00:32:29,581 --> 00:32:30,580
with sophistication.

410
00:32:30,581 --> 00:32:37,920
What we've got here is a P&H 4100A shovel with a 58 cubic yard dipper on it.

411
00:32:37,921 --> 00:32:42,240
This dipper's got a full lip of mechanically attached ground engaging tools on it.

412
00:32:42,241 --> 00:32:47,060
Inside the dipper you have an overlaid clad material, that's the shiny material you see

413
00:32:47,061 --> 00:32:48,060
in there.

414
00:32:48,061 --> 00:32:53,180
That allows the material to slide out and also protect the dipper from wear.

415
00:32:53,181 --> 00:32:56,060
The door itself weighs about 35,000 pounds.

416
00:32:56,760 --> 00:33:01,100
So as you're closing this door, there's quite a bit of force generated.

417
00:33:04,920 --> 00:33:10,180
At P&H Manufacturing, building the right type of dipper is an exacting process.

418
00:33:10,920 --> 00:33:13,540
It begins with on-site research and model testing.

419
00:33:14,920 --> 00:33:19,380
Actual scale models are built and then tested in a simulated miniature mine.

420
00:33:19,381 --> 00:33:28,020
Designs determine the right dipper shape and style, then 21st century engineering and some powerful software take over.

421
00:33:29,380 --> 00:33:34,560
Finite element analysis is a mathematical computer model of the proposed dipper's stiffness.

422
00:33:36,200 --> 00:33:41,420
Engineers apply simulated loads to predict the stresses that the steel will encounter while shoveling.

423
00:33:41,421 --> 00:33:51,460
With a final blueprint for various steel thicknesses, shapes, angles, and pitches, this double XL shovel becomes a reality.

424
00:33:52,900 --> 00:33:57,840
This is the back half of a 40 cubic yard dipper that's going to go to an iron ore mine.

425
00:33:58,480 --> 00:34:01,840
There'll be another half just like this that gets welded to this.

426
00:34:02,420 --> 00:34:05,900
And as you can see, this is a very thick plate on the side of this dipper.

427
00:34:06,920 --> 00:34:08,240
This is the lip on the dipper.

428
00:34:08,640 --> 00:34:10,000
This is a very heavy casting.

429
00:34:10,001 --> 00:34:14,440
It's made out of manganese steel, which work hardens as it goes through the bank.

430
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:17,420
This is the part of the lift that gets welded onto the dipper.

431
00:34:18,120 --> 00:34:22,220
When the pieces come together, the dipper is a formidable shovel.

432
00:34:22,940 --> 00:34:26,120
It has to be working the no-lunch-break, 24-hour shift.

433
00:34:26,680 --> 00:34:30,620
But just like humans, all dippers require regular visits with the dentist.

434
00:34:31,340 --> 00:34:35,100
What we're looking at here is the front edge of the dipper lift.

435
00:34:35,520 --> 00:34:38,040
And on the front edge of the dipper lift, you have the mining tooth.

436
00:34:38,041 --> 00:34:43,160
This is the primary ground engaging part on the whole system.

437
00:34:43,740 --> 00:34:47,380
Below that you have the adapter, which retains the tooth.

438
00:34:47,740 --> 00:34:50,100
The teeth are expendable. We go through a lot of teeth.

439
00:34:51,060 --> 00:34:51,760
At U.S.

440
00:34:51,761 --> 00:34:56,680
Borax, teeth wear more slowly because the shovel is excavating a softer shale material.

441
00:34:58,320 --> 00:35:00,420
But a new set of chompers is inevitable.

442
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:05,704
We're putting on the new teeth. They're a cast tooth.

443
00:35:05,705 --> 00:35:07,540
They weigh about 210 pounds a piece.

444
00:35:08,040 --> 00:35:13,140
Over the life of the tooth, they'll probably get about 500,000 tons of material.

445
00:35:13,860 --> 00:35:15,640
They're very simple to install.

446
00:35:16,840 --> 00:35:19,840
But one size certainly does not fit all.

447
00:35:20,700 --> 00:35:25,160
That's where an outfit like California's Berkeley Forge and Tool Company steps in.

448
00:35:26,180 --> 00:35:29,720
What we're looking at here is a variety of different mining teeth.

449
00:35:29,721 --> 00:35:37,380
They range in profile and weights, and that's all dependent on the size of the dipper, the power of the dipper.

450
00:35:38,040 --> 00:35:40,640
the muck that it's shoveling.

451
00:35:41,360 --> 00:35:44,380
For instance, here you have a very narrow profile,

452
00:35:44,920 --> 00:35:46,760
which would probably be used maybe for overburden.

453
00:35:47,700 --> 00:35:51,200
Something like this might be used for more of a hard rock application.

454
00:35:52,200 --> 00:35:55,120
Berkeley Forge is a rare supplier of mining teeth

455
00:35:55,121 --> 00:35:58,300
because it actually forges them by hammering the steel.

456
00:35:59,700 --> 00:36:02,000
Forging produces a super hard tooth.

457
00:36:03,260 --> 00:36:04,760
To shape these metal incisors,

458
00:36:04,761 --> 00:36:08,800
the raw steel is first heated to 2250 degrees Fahrenheit.

459
00:36:10,100 --> 00:36:12,960
One by one, each tooth meets its maker.

460
00:36:14,520 --> 00:36:17,020
Berkeley's 10,000-pound steam-powered hammer

461
00:36:17,021 --> 00:36:20,520
delivers several well-placed blows to form the tooth's shape.

462
00:36:21,660 --> 00:36:23,960
A second hammer forms the back extrusion,

463
00:36:24,380 --> 00:36:26,220
where it will mount onto the tooth adapter.

464
00:36:28,200 --> 00:36:32,580
The tooth adapters are formed in a 1,500-ton hydraulic press

465
00:36:32,581 --> 00:36:35,780
and precision milled for a perfect fit with the teeth.

466
00:36:37,500 --> 00:36:42,160
To guarantee the ultimate tooth, technicians perform a MagnaFlux inspection

467
00:36:42,161 --> 00:36:46,460
using ultraviolet light to search for cracks and tiny imperfections.

468
00:36:47,520 --> 00:36:51,420
The life expectancy on mining teeth is extremely volatile.

469
00:36:51,880 --> 00:36:54,840
You can have some mining applications where the muck is pretty easy

470
00:36:54,841 --> 00:36:58,080
and you can have a tooth last you for a month to two months.

471
00:36:58,520 --> 00:37:01,860
You can have some severe applications where they'll maybe go through one or two teeth

472
00:37:01,861 --> 00:37:03,300
in an eight-hour shift.

473
00:37:03,740 --> 00:37:06,740
I mean, all depends on the type of terrain that you're digging

474
00:37:06,741 --> 00:37:08,840
and the intensity that you're mining with.

475
00:37:09,700 --> 00:37:11,900
When these teeth meet the mine bank,

476
00:37:12,280 --> 00:37:14,440
even the biggest rocks don't stand a chance.

477
00:37:16,600 --> 00:37:18,400
The mining industry is booming.

478
00:37:19,620 --> 00:37:22,500
Manufacturers can barely keep up with the demand for shovels.

479
00:37:23,000 --> 00:37:24,460
But even at full capacity,

480
00:37:25,080 --> 00:37:30,060
P&H Mining Company is limited to producing 20 to 26 large machines in one year.

481
00:37:31,860 --> 00:37:37,180
And even when a shovel is complete, the bill of sales specifies some assembly required.

482
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:48,120
As large as our factory is and as high as our buildings are, it is impossible for us to assemble these shovels here, much less we wouldn't want to anyway.

483
00:37:48,300 --> 00:37:51,620
There's no way to ship an entire mining shovel from the factory to the mine.

484
00:37:51,621 --> 00:37:57,040
To get to a location like the bottom of the 700-foot-deep Borax Mine in the Mojave Desert,

485
00:37:57,840 --> 00:38:01,960
the shovel pieces are shipped in more than 40 railcars and semi-trucks.

486
00:38:02,460 --> 00:38:04,560
The puzzle is assembled in place.

487
00:38:05,560 --> 00:38:13,060
Over a period of two months, some 14 well-equipped specialists erect the nearly 3 million-pound, 7-story tall beast.

488
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:18,980
This shovel will dig for years, but it will never leave the mine.

489
00:38:18,981 --> 00:38:25,180
Another shovel is designed to never leave its digging zone millions of miles away.

490
00:38:25,900 --> 00:38:26,700
The Red Planet.

491
00:38:28,460 --> 00:38:35,540
A PNH 4100 shovel is capable of digging more than 35 million cubic yards in a single year.

492
00:38:35,900 --> 00:38:38,920
A volume more than 10 times that of Egypt's Great Pyramid.

493
00:38:40,340 --> 00:38:43,140
Thousands of years of digging have proven one thing.

494
00:38:43,840 --> 00:38:45,500
The shovel is indispensable.

495
00:38:46,280 --> 00:38:47,240
On planet Earth?

496
00:38:48,980 --> 00:38:50,660
and beyond.

497
00:38:51,220 --> 00:38:53,760
From the first of the unmanned moon missions,

498
00:38:54,660 --> 00:38:57,600
shovels have helped uncover the mysteries of our solar system.

499
00:38:58,920 --> 00:39:00,320
In April 1967,

500
00:39:01,160 --> 00:39:06,120
the Surveyor 3 lander arrived on the moon with a robotic arm and shovel attachment.

501
00:39:07,100 --> 00:39:09,420
The scoop tested the depth of the lunar dust

502
00:39:09,421 --> 00:39:11,880
to show that manned missions could land safely.

503
00:39:14,780 --> 00:39:18,500
Several hand shovels made their lunar debut with the Apollo astronauts,

504
00:39:18,980 --> 00:39:21,380
to collect moon rocks.

505
00:39:21,381 --> 00:39:25,980
In 1976, Viking 1 and Viking 2 landed on Mars,

506
00:39:25,981 --> 00:39:28,420
toting robotic shovels.

507
00:39:28,421 --> 00:39:32,600
The soil samples they extracted tantalized scientists.

508
00:39:32,601 --> 00:39:35,000
Later missions, such as the Odyssey Orbiter

509
00:39:35,001 --> 00:39:37,500
and the Spirit and Opportunity rovers,

510
00:39:37,501 --> 00:39:41,800
confirmed the existence of water on Mars.

511
00:39:41,801 --> 00:39:44,540
Now it's up to the Jet Propulsion Laboratory's Phoenix

512
00:39:44,541 --> 00:39:47,180
lander and its sophisticated shovel

513
00:39:47,181 --> 00:39:50,220
to try to uncover evidence of life on Mars.

514
00:39:50,840 --> 00:39:54,160
What Phoenix is really going to do is to go up to the northern latitudes

515
00:39:54,161 --> 00:39:57,400
where we know the water actually still exists, albeit in ice form,

516
00:39:57,660 --> 00:39:59,940
and dig into it with our robotic arm.

517
00:40:00,920 --> 00:40:04,640
The holy grail, so to speak, is if we would find organics in the measurement,

518
00:40:04,800 --> 00:40:06,260
which would really be a sign of life.

519
00:40:07,140 --> 00:40:11,680
But getting that robotic shovel to the surface of Mars is a formidable task.

520
00:40:12,200 --> 00:40:13,840
When it hits the Martian atmosphere,

521
00:40:13,841 --> 00:40:17,840
The lander will be traveling at nearly 12,000 miles an hour.

522
00:40:17,841 --> 00:40:23,840
Atmospheric drag in a parachute will help ensure a safe arrival for the lander and its precious shovel.

523
00:40:23,841 --> 00:40:31,840
This lander is actually going to use pulse thrusters to slow its descent down and land itself on the surface in what we call a soft landing.

524
00:40:31,841 --> 00:40:34,840
No more airbags and bouncing for this vehicle.

525
00:40:34,841 --> 00:40:39,840
Once the solar panels are deployed, the Phoenix's robotic arm and shovel can get to work.

526
00:40:39,841 --> 00:40:40,541
work.

527
00:40:40,542 --> 00:40:46,200
This is a full-scale model of the Phoenix Lander, and as you can see, the robotic arm is

528
00:40:46,201 --> 00:40:48,384
here in the stowed configuration.

529
00:40:48,385 --> 00:40:52,620
The arm itself has what we call four degrees of freedom, or four

530
00:40:52,621 --> 00:40:56,032
active joints, and we use those to deploy the arm.

531
00:40:56,033 --> 00:40:59,240
As you can see, what we'll do is we'll actually

532
00:40:59,241 --> 00:41:04,037
lift it up. One of the degrees of freedom is at the shoulder that lifts it up.

533
00:41:04,038 --> 00:41:04,960
We also have an

534
00:41:04,961 --> 00:41:07,500
azimuth joint at the shoulder, so we have two over here.

535
00:41:07,501 --> 00:41:09,360
We have an elbow joint, just like a human

536
00:41:09,361 --> 00:41:13,320
arm at the top and a degree of freedom at the end for the scoop for the shovel.

537
00:41:13,321 --> 00:41:19,080
Using a rotary attachment on the head, the shovel will first grind into the hard, icy

538
00:41:19,081 --> 00:41:19,863
topsoil.

539
00:41:19,864 --> 00:41:27,500
Once past the first layer, the scoop will utilize ripper tines or sharp blades

540
00:41:27,501 --> 00:41:29,632
to trench the surface.

541
00:41:29,633 --> 00:41:36,220
A camera mounted on the robotic arm will help JPL scientists observe

542
00:41:36,221 --> 00:41:37,120
and direct the digging.

543
00:41:38,400 --> 00:41:41,080
At a distance of 35 million miles,

544
00:41:41,800 --> 00:41:44,600
this is one shovel that millions of people will be watching.

545
00:41:45,000 --> 00:41:47,000
It is absolutely the first time we're using

546
00:41:47,001 --> 00:41:50,060
such a highly complicated mechanism of this sort

547
00:41:50,061 --> 00:41:51,480
on the surface of another planet.

548
00:41:52,140 --> 00:41:54,540
The Phoenix won't be the only robotic shovel

549
00:41:54,541 --> 00:41:56,380
in the solar system. Far from it.

550
00:41:57,320 --> 00:42:00,380
Back on Earth, robotic shovels are saving lives.

551
00:42:02,280 --> 00:42:03,980
From up to two miles away,

552
00:42:03,981 --> 00:42:06,980
the U.S. Air Force's automated ordnance excavator

553
00:42:06,981 --> 00:42:10,220
can locate and safely remove unexploded bombs.

554
00:42:12,180 --> 00:42:16,500
The operator controls all functions of the excavator via radio frequency.

555
00:42:21,080 --> 00:42:22,780
Thousands of miles away,

556
00:42:23,380 --> 00:42:26,660
this modified Hitachi excavator is sweeping for landmines.

557
00:42:29,280 --> 00:42:32,380
Every year, thousands of people are maimed or killed

558
00:42:32,381 --> 00:42:34,040
by anti-personnel landmines.

559
00:42:35,840 --> 00:42:40,140
In front of the shovel face, a rotary cutter head churns the earth

560
00:42:40,141 --> 00:42:42,260
as it aims to trip the buried mines.

561
00:42:45,080 --> 00:42:48,440
The mechanism is designed to resist searing blasts

562
00:42:48,441 --> 00:42:50,440
of up to 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

563
00:42:51,520 --> 00:42:54,280
The shovel itself protects the operator from flying debris.

564
00:42:56,340 --> 00:43:01,940
In 2004, these demining shovels detonated approximately 4,000 landmines

565
00:43:01,941 --> 00:43:05,400
in Afghanistan and 8,000 in Nicaragua.

566
00:43:07,440 --> 00:43:12,200
For centuries, whatever the job, the shovel has adapted to fit the need.

567
00:43:13,220 --> 00:43:16,280
Its legacy even inspires a gleam of patriotism.

568
00:43:17,020 --> 00:43:18,960
These products literally built America.

569
00:43:19,140 --> 00:43:22,400
I mean, it's sort of like a catchphrase, you know, X or Y built America,

570
00:43:22,401 --> 00:43:24,380
but here's something that literally did.

571
00:43:25,040 --> 00:43:27,080
Not only America, but the world.

572
00:43:27,081 --> 00:43:34,080
Shovels have turned the earth in search of riches and dug the foundations of our societies.

573
00:43:34,081 --> 00:43:40,080
Judge their importance by the fact that as ceremonial tools dipped in gold or silver,

574
00:43:40,081 --> 00:43:44,080
shovels are the very symbol of human achievement.

